  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What anonymous actually means</h2>
      <p class="reveal">Removing names does not make a dataset anonymous, and the word is often used for data that is not. The UK Information Commissioner&rsquo;s Office notes that datasets are commonly described as anonymised when they still hold personal data in pseudonymised form, and that anonymising personal data is itself a form of processing.</p>
      <p class="reveal">The test is whether re-identification is reasonably likely, not whether it is merely conceivable. Rare events, small groups and the surrounding context can identify people after the names are gone, which is why personal information gets its own review, separate from rights, and why a scope can be narrowed or declined.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Questions about your data?</h2>
      <p class="reveal">DataLeasers introduces companies to a data buyer. The agreement is between your company and that buyer, and it pays you directly.</p>
      <p class="reveal">Questions about the agreements, your data rights or how the data is used go to the data buyer, and your own counsel can review the terms.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

